## First principles: three facts

### 1. SweetAlert2 is just JavaScript that draws a modal

It is an npm package (`sweetalert2`). Vite bundles it into `site.js`. It is not part of Django.

When JS calls `Swal.fire({ ... })`, the library creates a dialog in the page and fills it from the options you pass. Two of those options matter:

| Option | What SweetAlert does with the string |
|---|---|
| `text:` | Puts it in a **text node**. Angle brackets show as characters. |
| `html:` | Feeds it to the browser’s **HTML parser** (same family of operation as `innerHTML`). Tags become elements. `onerror=` on an image runs. |

That is the whole library, for this bug: a function that can either display a string or execute it as markup.

### 2. This project never calls `window.confirm()`

The agent built a small wrapper so every “are you sure?” dialog looks like SweetAlert.

Load path:

```text
templates/web/base.html
  {% vite_asset 'assets/javascript/site.js' %}
        ↓
assets/javascript/site.js
  import './confirm';
        ↓
assets/javascript/confirm.js
  import Swal from 'sweetalert2';
```

`confirm.js` does three things:

1. Read the message off the clicked element: `element.dataset.confirmMessage`
2. Open SweetAlert with that string in `html:`
3. If the user clicks Confirm, continue the original action (HTMX request or form submit)

Templates opt in with HTML attributes. They do not call SweetAlert themselves:

```html
data-confirm-title="Confirm Role Change"
data-confirm-message="Change <strong>{{ entry.user.get_display_name }}</strong> from <strong>Admin</strong> to <strong>Manager</strong>?"
data-confirm-btn="Yes, change"
```

`data-confirm-message` becomes `element.dataset.confirmMessage` in JS (hyphens become camelCase). That is the only bridge between Django and SweetAlert.

### 3. Django auto-escape only protects the **first** HTML parse

`{{ entry.user.get_display_name }}` is auto-escaped. If the name contains `<`, Django writes `&lt;` into the HTML source. That stops the name from becoming a tag **while the browser is building the staff-list page**.

It does not freeze the string as escaped forever. When JS later reads `dataset.confirmMessage`, the browser gives back the **decoded** original, including the `<`.

So: Django escaped for parse #1. JS + SweetAlert then run parse #2 on the decoded value.

