## How to recognise this class of bug

Ask one question whenever user text meets JavaScript:

**Does this string get parsed as HTML a second time?**

Sinks that mean “yes”:

- SweetAlert `html:` (and `title:` / `title` as HTML)
- `element.innerHTML = ...`
- `insertAdjacentHTML`
- Alpine `x-html`
- jQuery `.html()`

Sources that look safe and are not, once they hit those sinks:

- `{{ value }}` inside `data-*="..."` then `element.dataset.*`
- `{{ value }}` inside `onclick="Swal.fire({ html: '...' })"`
- JSON in a `<script>` type that you later pass to `html:`

Django `|escape` / auto-escape does not close those. `|escapejs` is for embedding into a JS **literal** in the template. It does not help a value that is read from `dataset`.

The tell in this codebase is specifically:

```text
template:  data-confirm-message="... {{ user-controlled }} ..."
     +
confirm.js:  html: confirmMessage(element)
```

Any `data-confirm-message` / `data-confirm-body` / `data-confirm-title` that interpolates `{{ }}` is in that family. Static sentences (submit journal, cancel receipt, and so on) are not XSS, because there is no user string to parse.

