You need the user-controlled part to become a **text node**, and the bold labels to stay out of that string.

The finding’s approach:

1. In `showConfirm`, pass the message as `text:` instead of `html:`. SweetAlert will not parse tags in the name.
2. Stop putting `<strong>` in `data-confirm-message`. Bold “Admin” in the template is what forced `html:` in the first place. Put emphasis in the static title, or build the dialog body in JS with `document.createTextNode(name)` plus your own `<strong>` for the known words.
3. Optionally reject `<` and `>` in `first_name` / `last_name` so the payload cannot even be stored. That is defense in depth. The dialog still has to use `text:`, because names can be changed in the admin or a future form.
