## The precise rule

User-controlled text (from the DB, from a form, from a query string) is data. It becomes an XSS bug only at a **sink**: a place that tells the browser “this string is markup” or “this string is code.”

| What you do with the string | What the browser does | Result |
|---|---|---|
| Put it in a Django text node: `{{ name }}` | Shows characters | Safe (auto-escaped) |
| Put it in `data-*`, then read `dataset` and pass to SweetAlert `text:` | Shows characters | Safe |
| Put it in `data-*`, then pass to SweetAlert `html:` | Parses tags | XSS if the string contains tags |
| `element.textContent = name` | Shows characters | Safe |
| `element.innerHTML = name` | Parses tags | XSS |
| `eval(name)` / `new Function(name)` | Runs as JS | XSS |

F2 is the third row. The name sat in the DB as a harmless string. It sat in `dataset` as a harmless string. SweetAlert `html:` is what promoted it to HTML.

So: **storage is fine. A JS variable is fine. Interpretation as HTML/JS is the bug.**

---

## Why “anything from the view/DB” is too wide

Almost every screen in Spicy shows DB data: item names, cashier names, order numbers, warehouse names. Those are supposed to reach the browser.

This is safe:

```html
