```

This is also safe:

```javascript
Swal.fire({ text: element.dataset.confirmMessage });
```

Same DB value, same trip through JS. The dialog shows the characters `<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>` and nothing runs.

The staff-list table already does the safe version of this. Only the confirm dialog did the unsafe version.

---

## A better question to ask every time

When you see user text heading toward JS, ask:

**Will this string be inserted as text, or parsed as HTML/code?**

- Text APIs: `textContent`, `innerText`, SweetAlert `text:`, Django `{{ }}` in a text node, `document.createTextNode`
- Parse APIs: `innerHTML`, `insertAdjacentHTML`, SweetAlert `html:` / `title`, Alpine `x-html`, `document.write`, `eval`

If it is a parse API, Django having escaped it in the template does not save you, because `dataset` (and JSON, and many other reads) give you the original characters back.

That is the whole class: **untrusted string + HTML/JS parser.** The database is only how the string survived until a privileged user opened the page.

