## The rewrite that follows that rule

The bug was one shared sink. Every confirm dialog in the app goes through `confirm.js`. Fixing that file once is the practice; chasing each template is how the next name field gets missed.

**1. The wrapper now uses text APIs**

`showConfirm` used to pass the dataset string to `html:`. It now passes it to `text:` and `titleText:`.

That covers every `data-confirm-*` dialog: staff roles, menu item remove, GL mapping delete, cash-out, journals, stock documents, P&L submit, and the rest. Those templates can still interpolate `{{ name }}`. Django escapes the attribute for the page. SweetAlert then displays the decoded characters as text.

**2. Markup left the user-data path**

Staff-list messages had hardcoded `<strong>` around the name and the role so `html:` would bold them. With `text:`, those tags would have shown on screen as the characters `<strong>`. They are gone. The sentence is now plain:

`Change {{ entry.user.get_display_name }} from Admin to Manager?`

**3. Toasts follow the same rule**

Django messages can include `get_display_name()`. `toast.js` used `title:`, which SweetAlert parses as HTML. It now uses `titleText:`.

Menu item names and payment-mode names in confirm attributes needed no template rewrite. They were already plain sentences. The wrapper was the hole.

